  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1120 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nAbove normal temperatures will continue to impact the Mid-South\nthrough Saturday as the upper level ridge shifts east and a\ntropical maritime airmass moves in. High temperatures will be\n10 to 15 degrees above normal, in the low and mid 70s. Despite\nthis, it appears we will not set any high temperature records\nover the next several days as forecast highs remain several\ndegrees below record breaking levels.\n\nAn active synoptic pattern will manifest on Sunday as a shortwave\nand attendant cold front impact the Mid-South. Ahead of FROPA,\nprecipitable water values will surge to the 90th percentile. The\nboundary will provide just enough lift to encourage shower\ndevelopment by the afternoon hours. The RRFS-MPAS depicts MUCAPE\nvalues reaching 200 J/kg along and east of the Mississippi River\nSunday afternoon. Given poor overlap in other severe weather\nparameters, no organized thunderstorms are anticipated. Instead,\nexpect light showers with a low (~20%) chance for a few rumbles\nof thunder. Showers will dissipate by Monday morning, leaving\nbehind rainfall totals of less than a quarter of an inch.\n\nSeasonable temperatures arrive Monday as cool and dry air filters\ninto the Mid-South. There are still run-to-run model\ninconsistencies regarding placement and timing of a closed upper\nlevel low by midweek. For now, it is safe to say additional\nrounds of rain are not out of the question as we move towards\nThanksgiving.",
